======================================================================
Route A: E[M(c)^2] directly from M(c) = min(1, sqrt(E/c)), E~Exp(1)
======================================================================
term1 = int_0^c (e/c)e^-e de = (-c + exp(c) - 1)*exp(-c)/c
term2 = int_c^oo e^-e de     = exp(-c)
E[M(c)^2] (route A)          = (exp(c) - 1)*exp(-c)/c
Candidate closed form (1-e^-c)/c matches route A exactly: True  (diff = 0 )

======================================================================
Route B: E[M(c)^2] via the K-mixture definition of Conjecture 2
  (M(c) := M_K, K~Poisson(c) independent; use Conjecture 1's
   f_{M_K}(x) = 2Kx(1-x^2)^{K-1} -- CONJECTURED for K>=2, PROVED
   K=1,2,3 -- to get E[M_K^2] for each K, then Poisson-mix.)
======================================================================
E[M_K^2] (from Conjecture 1's density, symbolic in K) = 1/(Kint + 1)
K=0 special case: M_0 = 1 a.s. => E[M_0^2] = 1 (not covered by the K>=1 formula above)
Simplified E[M_K^2] as function of K: 1/(Kint + 1)
Poisson-mixture sum (route B), closed form: (exp(c) - 1)*exp(-c)/c
Matches (1-e^-c)/c exactly: True  (diff = 0 )

